    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d165">

                <pb facs="0408" n="386" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg386"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">781.13/7–2650</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Minor</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                        <lb/> [No.] 147</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-07-26">July 26, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Enclosed is a memorandum<anchor n="1" xml:id="d165-f1"/> of my recent
                    conversation with the Greek Prime Minister, General Nicholas Plastiras.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d165-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>The Department may be as discouraged as I was to learn that the Greeks are again
                    thinking of additional foreign assistance as the only way out of Greece’s
                    economic difficulties.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d165-f3"/> We will make quite clear
                    on every appropriate occasion our disagreement with this attitude.</p>
                <p>It will be noted that although the Greek Prime Minister emphasized his personal
                    conviction that widest possible leniency measures should be adopted, he also
                    stated that this development would probably not occur as the other government
                    leaders disagreed with him on this point. Later the same day, Plastiras issued a
                    press release indicating that international developments precluded any
                    considerable extension of leniency measures. (Embassy despatch 146 of July
                        26.)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d165-f4"/>
                </p>
                <pb facs="0409" n="387" xml:id="pg387"/>
                <p>In general, I must remark that the Prime Minister’s views on both economic and
                    security questions indicated a lamentable lack of realism.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harold</hi> B. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Minor</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d165-f1" n="1" xml:id="d165-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d165-f2" n="2" xml:id="d165-f2-content">
                <p>Also present at this meeting, held on July 24 at Prime Minister Plastiras’
                    request in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, were John Moatsis, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Chef de Cabinet</hi> of the Prime Minister, Stephen T.
                    Calligas of the Embassy in Greece, and Robert G. Miner, Second Secretary of
                    Embassy and Consul at Athens.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d165-f3" n="3" xml:id="d165-f3-content">
                <p>Chargé in Greece Harold B. Minor reported that Prime Minister Plastiras “thought
                    that the economic difficulties of Greece might be alleviated by a loan of 50 to
                    60 million dollars by the International Bank” (enclosure to source text, not
                    printed).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d165-f4" n="1" xml:id="d165-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
